WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.143
An artifact, built in a chat.

00:00:02.143 --> 00:00:03.837
One click on Install as app.

00:00:04.217 --> 00:00:07.376
Now it opens in its own window, with its own icon, like

00:00:07.376 --> 00:00:08.955
any other app on the desk.

00:00:11.290 --> 00:00:12.122
Hi, I'm Trolley.

00:00:12.122 --> 00:00:15.729
Two launches on one evening, both about what a web page can be.

00:00:19.942 --> 00:00:21.511
What the app may touch is up to you.

00:00:22.253 --> 00:00:25.793
By default an installed app can do only what a browser page can.

00:00:26.213 --> 00:00:30.503
It can ask for more: notifications, global shortcuts, a menu bar item.

00:00:30.923 --> 00:00:34.086
Or full access to run programs and change files.

00:00:34.086 --> 00:00:37.601
Claude asks again after every update, even your own edits.

00:00:39.367 --> 00:00:42.156
One person posted sixty-one apps made this way.

00:00:42.156 --> 00:00:43.551
A crystal plate scorer.

00:00:43.551 --> 00:00:44.597
A knitting chart.

00:00:44.977 --> 00:00:46.796
A glacier traced year by year.

00:00:46.796 --> 00:00:48.918
A sailing passage with a tide gate.

00:00:48.918 --> 00:00:50.736
Each one a dense, single window.

00:00:52.742 --> 00:00:56.799
The page registers its functions as tools on document model context.

00:00:56.799 --> 00:01:01.962
Before each request, the browser lists them and sends the definitions to your server.

00:01:02.382 --> 00:01:04.668
The model runs on the server, key and all.

00:01:04.668 --> 00:01:07.463
It answers with a tool call, but the server has no

00:01:07.463 --> 00:01:08.479
way to run it.

00:01:08.899 --> 00:01:09.871
The browser does.

00:01:09.871 --> 00:01:13.759
The call goes through the MCP client and its WebMCP transport into

00:01:13.759 --> 00:01:17.322
the page's own function, and two mugs land in the cart.

00:01:17.742 --> 00:01:21.228
The result goes back as structured content, and the chat sends itself

00:01:21.228 --> 00:01:22.680
again for the next step.

00:01:24.367 --> 00:01:27.088
In the AI SDK it is one transport.

00:01:27.088 --> 00:01:29.129
Pass it the page's model context.

00:01:29.509 --> 00:01:30.713
List the tools, call one.

00:01:30.713 --> 00:01:33.361
There is no server on the other end of this wire.

00:01:34.992 --> 00:01:36.602
Add two mugs and a tote bag.

00:01:37.665 --> 00:01:38.450
Clear the cart.

00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:40.020
Add three T-shirts and a mug.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:41.210
Place the order.

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:44.450
The page marked that button for a person, so it asks first.

00:01:44.830 --> 00:01:46.467
No tool was written for any of it.

00:01:48.067 --> 00:01:52.837
WindTunnel's sites wrote their tools by hand: over seven thousand lines for forty-four tools.

00:01:52.837 --> 00:01:56.584
One of them finishes a whole checkout in a single call.

00:01:57.004 --> 00:02:00.535
UI SDK registers three generic tools for the whole page.

00:02:00.535 --> 00:02:04.773
Nothing to write, but the model plans every step from the labels.

00:02:06.142 --> 00:02:08.717
An installed app needs permissions you can take back.

00:02:08.717 --> 00:02:11.291
A page full of tools needs an allow callback.

00:02:11.291 --> 00:02:13.294
The same page can now have both.

00:02:14.942 --> 00:02:17.066
A window and permissions from Claude.

00:02:17.066 --> 00:02:19.191
Tools in the tab through WebMCP.

00:02:19.191 --> 00:02:21.669
Three of them free from UI SDK.

00:02:22.089 --> 00:02:24.152
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:24.152 --> 00:02:24.742
I'm Trolley.

00:02:24.742 --> 00:02:25.037
Bye!
